You are asking another repo for something. REQ is the reactive channel along the hierarchy: client → server, server → microservice, microservice → its parent server. The recipient owes an answer.

- A REQ arrived for you → `sdh-cross-repo-req-answer`. A push with no answer expected → `sdh-cross-repo-announce-send`. Two repos on the same level (client↔client, server↔server, ms↔ms) → `sdh-peer-channel-send`.
- Solo Dev Hub moves files between repos. You never copy, move or delete across a repo boundary; you write only in your own outgoing folder.

## Files

- `REQ-NNN_<slug>.md` — yours, the source of truth; the recipient holds a replica.
- `REQ-NNN_<slug>.response.md` — the recipient's receipt; your copy is a replica, never edit it.
- `NNN` is never renamed or reused. The slug is optional (the app matches `REQ-*.md`).
- Revising your REQ before a receipt: edit the same file in place, never a second file under the same number.

## 1. Number — from the registry

| You | Registry |
|---|---|
| Client → server | `docs/backend-requirements/registry.md` |
| Server → microservice | `docs/microservice-requirements/registry.md` |

- New number = `max` over the registry + 1, three digits. One counter per channel, across all counterparties.
- Registry missing or empty → seed it first from every `REQ-*.md` in the channel, all subfolders; nothing anywhere → `001`. Never take the number from the folder listing: closed pairs are deleted.
- Write the opening line in the same pass as the REQ: `- REQ-014 | out | swan_code_server | pagination on /orders | 2026-08-24 | | |` — fields `id | direction | counterpart | subject | opened | closed | final status | where the result went`. Direction is `out` here. The app never writes or deletes the registry.

## 2. Folder

| You | Outgoing folder |
|---|---|
| Client → server | `docs/backend-requirements/` |
| Server → microservice | `docs/microservice-requirements/<microservice-project-name>/` |

Never rename sync folders — the app handles renames.

## 3. Body

```markdown
# REQ-NNN: <one-line title>

## Description
What is needed and why — the problem, not just "do X".

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] observable condition
- [ ] observable condition

## Context (optional)
Links, constraints, related endpoints or tables.
```

- Phrase it in the recipient's contract: a server asks a microservice for what the server needs, never "the client wants X".
- If audience (admin vs user) matters for access, say it in the body.

## 4. Delivery

- The next sync delivers the REQ and, later, returns the receipt. Your later edits overwrite the recipient's copy.
- UI states: `new` → `sent` → `responded`.
- A receipt or announcement that unblocks a task: remove the blocked note in `docs/todo.md` in the same pass and tell the owner which release it now sits in.

## 5. Closing

- **✓ in the app**, or **drop an empty `REQ-NNN_<slug>.impl.md`** beside your REQ when you are done integrating. The next sync deletes `.md`, `.response.md` and `.impl.md` on both sides.
- Fill the registry line (closed date, final status, where the result went) in the same pass. Closed by ✓ → fill it the next time you touch the channel.
- **After dropping `.impl.md`, touch nothing.** Deleting any of the files yourself before a sync processes the close makes the next sync re-send your REQ as open, with no receipt.
- Unsatisfied with a final receipt (`implemented` / `partially` / `declined`): write `REQ-N+1` with the clarified ask.
- Receipt is `clarification-needed`: answer inline in the **original** REQ; the recipient overwrites its receipt.
- Withdrawing an open REQ: put `**WITHDRAWN <date>:** <why>` under its H1 and keep the body; close with `.impl.md` once any receipt lands.

## Downstream REQ (server forwarding a client REQ to a microservice)

An ordinary send, plus:
1. Reformulate in server↔microservice terms.
2. Right after the H1: `**Forwarded-from:** <client-repo-last-segment>/REQ-NNN` (e.g. `web-app-client/REQ-001`). One client REQ forwarded to several microservices → the header in each.

When to forward and the client receipt: `sdh-cross-repo-req-answer`, "Forwarding".

## Never

- Write a `REQ-*.md` into an incoming folder (a server writing in `docs/client-requirements/` — server-to-client goes through announcements).
- Edit a `.response.md`, delete pair files by hand, renumber, rename sync folders.
